'''Greggory D. Hull''' is an American politician and businessman who served as mayor of [[Rio Rancho, New Mexico]] from 2014 to April 2026,<ref name="sfnm">{{cite news |title=Rio Rancho mayor becomes first Republican to enter gubernatorial race |url=https://www.santafenewmexican.com/news/local_news/rio-rancho-mayor-becomes-first-republican-to-enter-gubernatorial-race/article_082e2704-9dca-4db2-aec2-2b3e839fa720.html |work=[[The Santa Fe New Mexican]] |date=April 16, 2025|last=Chacon|first=Daniel}}</ref><ref name="kob">{{cite news |title=Rio Rancho mayor announces bid for New Mexico governor |url=https://www.kob.com/new-mexico/rio-rancho-mayor-announces-bid-for-new-mexico-governor/ |work=[[KOB (TV)|KOB]] |date=April 15, 2025}}</ref><ref name="rro-noseek">{{cite news |title=Hull will not seek re-election as Rio Rancho mayor |url=https://www.rrobserver.com/news/local-government/hull-will-not-seek-re-election-as-rio-rancho-mayor/article_3a18be7d-9b66-4236-aa55-12ec8cc43e35.html |work=Rio Rancho Observer |date=November 19, 2025}}</ref> the longest-serving mayor in Rio Rancho history.<ref name="rro">{{cite news |title=Hull celebrates a decade of leading Rio Rancho |url=https://www.rrobserver.com/news/hull-celebrates-a-decade-of-leading-rio-rancho/article_0d451056-f917-11ee-8bc8-d3587f5b1ae3.html |work=Rio Rancho Observer |date=April 20, 2024|last=Hendricks|first=Kevin}}</ref> Hull is the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] nominee for the [[Governor of New Mexico]] in the [[2026 New Mexico gubernatorial election|2026 election]].<ref name="sfnm" /><ref name="nmpol">{{cite news |title=Rio Rancho mayor officially enters 2026 governor's race |url=https://nmpoliticalreport.com/2025/10/15/rio-rancho-mayor-officially-enters-2026-governors-race/ |work=New Mexico Political Report |date=October 15, 2025}}</ref><ref name="NYTPrimary">{{cite news |last=Thebault |first=Reis |title=Gregg Hull Wins G.O.P. Nomination to Be New Mexico Governor |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/06/03/us/elections/new-mexico-republican-governor-hull.html|work=The New York Times |date=June 3, 2026 |access-date=July 22, 2026}}</ref><ref name="BallotpediaPrimary">{{cite web |title=New Mexico gubernatorial election, 2026 (June 2 Republican primary) |url=https://ballotpedia.org/New_Mexico_gubernatorial_election,_2026_(June_2_Republican_primary) |website=[[Ballotpedia]] |date=July 7, 2026 |access-date=July 22, 2026}}</ref><ref name="APPrimary">{{cite news |title=Former Interior Secretary Deb Haaland and ex-Mayor Gregg Hull to face off for New Mexico governor |url=https://apnews.com/article/new-mexico-primary-governor-native-american-oil-ba6180bc3b985783b7811d56822b6b11|work=Associated Press|date=June 2, 2026 |access-date=July 22, 2026}}</ref>

== Early life and career ==
Hull was born on {{birth based on age as of date|59|2026|5|11|noage=yes}} in [[Oklahoma]].<ref name=":0">{{Cite news |last=Boyd |first=Dan |date=May 11, 2026 |title=Rio Rancho's longest-serving mayor eyes governor's office |url=https://www.yahoo.com/news/articles/rio-ranchos-longest-serving-mayor-140600561.html |work=[[Albuquerque Journal]] |via=[[Yahoo News]]}}</ref> He has lived in New Mexico for more than 35 years and was a small businessman in Rio Rancho before serving as mayor.<ref name="rro" /><ref name="rrnm">{{cite web |title=Mayor Hull Biography |url=https://rrnm.gov/2277/Biography |publisher=City of Rio Rancho, New Mexico |access-date=March 3, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190821091255/https://rrnm.gov/2277/Biography|archive-date=August 21, 2019|url-status=dead}}</ref> He graduated from [[Pagosa Springs High School]] in Colorado.<ref name=":0" />

== Mayor of Rio Rancho ==
Hull was first elected in 2014 in a runoff election and was re-elected in both 2018 and 2022.<ref name="rro" /><ref name="rrnm" /> Rio Rancho, the state's third-largest city, was the fastest-growing city in New Mexico while he was mayor.<ref name="sfnm" /><ref name="rro" /> While he was mayor, Hull doubled the city's police budget and pursued pro-business economic development policies.<ref name="nmpol" /> In November 2025, he announced he would not seek a fourth term in order to focus on his gubernatorial campaign.<ref name="rro-noseek" />

== 2026 gubernatorial campaign ==
{{Main|2026 New Mexico gubernatorial election}}

In April 2025, Hull became the first Republican to signal his intention to enter the race for governor.<ref name="sfnm" /><ref name="kob" /> After a statewide listening tour over the following months, he officially launched his campaign in October 2025, running on a platform focusing on public safety and economic growth.<ref name="nmpol" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Lee |first=Morgan |date=October 17, 2025 |title=First Republican enters race for governor of New Mexico in 2026 as Democrat terms out of office |url=https://apnews.com/article/gregg-hull-new-mexico-governor-race-403ecec6734dfa6dc05c355395dc6fc9 |access-date=March 29, 2026 |website=AP News |language=en}}</ref>

In March 2026, the GOP pre-primary in [[Ruidoso, New Mexico|Ruidoso]] gave Hull the clear majority, with many observers declaring him the 'favorite' for the Republican nomination.<ref>{{Cite web |last=D’Ammassa |first=Algernon |date=March 8, 2026 |title=New Mexico GOP delegates weigh in on 2026 primary candidates |url=https://www.abqjournal.com/news/rio-rancho-mayor-favorite-for-governor-at-gop-pre-primary-convention/2996066 |access-date=March 29, 2026 |website=abqjournal |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Davis |first=Pat |date=March 9, 2026 |title=State Republicans pick Hull, Turner to lead '26 ticket |url=https://nmpoliticalreport.com/2026/03/09/state-republicans-pick-hull-turner-to-lead-26-ticket/ |access-date=March 29, 2026 |website=New Mexico Political Report |language=en-US}}</ref>

He won the Republican primary held on June 2 and will face Democrat [[Deb Haaland]] in the general election.<ref name="NYTPrimary" />

== Personal life ==
Hull married his first wife, Charlene, when he was 20 and she was 16 years old. <ref>{{Cite web |last=Simonich |first=Milan |date=2026-05-14 |title=Records show GOP candidate for governor lagged on child support |url=https://www.santafenewmexican.com/news/elections/records-show-gop-candidate-for-governor-lagged-on-child-support/article_15583bdd-b0c2-4cef-9b88-c21a37996cba.html |access-date=2026-09-26 |website=Santa Fe New Mexican |language=en}}</ref>

Hull and his 2nd wife Carrie have been married for more than 25 years and have five children and eight grandchildren. They live in Rio Rancho.<ref name="rrnm" />
